cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The ARGENI Tripwire: Ownerless Bait Keys as a Consensus-Level Q-Day Alarm (reference implementation)"
message: "If you use or build on the ARGENI Tripwire, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Pillas
    given-names: Ryan Paul G.
version: "0.2"
date-released: "2026-10-28"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ryanpaulpillas/argeni-chain"
abstract: >-
  Reference implementation of the ARGENI Tripwire, a consensus-level alarm for the day classical
  public-key cryptography is broken. The chain holds a ladder of ownerless bait keys (elliptic curves
  of 160 to 256 bits, including secp256k1, plus the RSA-2048 Challenge modulus), each with a bounty.
  Claims use commit-reveal; a verified break raises the chain's alert level and brings forward or
  enforces the retirement of classical signatures, leaving vulnerable accounts able only to migrate
  to post-quantum keys. Part of the ARGENI post-quantum blockchain (ML-DSA, SLH-DSA, ML-KEM, BFT).
keywords:
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - quantum canary
  - Q-Day
  - blockchain
  - Shor's algorithm
  - tripwire
